- **Toolbar** with Back and Forward buttons and an editable address bar.
- **New Tab button** (`+`) at the end of the notebook tab strip; new tabs open blank with the address bar focused.
- **Per-tab close button**, independent history, and tab labels that follow the page title (falling back to “New Tab”).
- **Address entry** accepts a bare host (`example.org` → `https://example.org/`), passes `file:`, `about:`, `data:`, and `view-source:` through, trims surrounding whitespace, and ignores an empty submit.

Structurally, [main.c](src/main.c) now owns only application startup, [browser.c](src/browser.c) owns the window, toolbar, notebook, and chrome synchronization, and [tab.c](src/tab.c)/[tab.h](src/tab.h) own the web view and title label. [meson.build](meson.build) builds all three units; the README describes the new behavior.

Playtesting found two genuine bugs, both fixed:

1. `switch-page` fires while `gtk_notebook_get_current_page()` still returns the previous tab, so switching tabs showed the old tab’s URL and history state. The handler now uses the signal’s page argument.
2. WebKitGTK 6.0 exposes `can_go_back`/`can_go_forward` only as methods — there are no notify-able properties (confirmed against the runtime property list) — so back/forward never became clickable. The buttons now refresh on `load-changed` COMMITTED/FINISHED.

Validation: `meson compile` is clean, `-Wall -Wextra -Werror` is clean, and a temporary harness drove 30 assertions through the real window — tab creation, blank state, URL normalization, whitespace/file URLs, history back/forward, tab switching, title updates, background/current tab closing, and closing the last tab ending the app. All passed; the harness was deleted. `project-indigo-builds/development/test.sh` still reconfigures, builds, and launches the updated binary, with a live `WebKitWebProcess`.

Known limits: there is no reload/stop button, no keyboard shortcuts, no search fallback for non-URL text, and no CEF tab yet. Nothing was committed.

A natural next pass is the small set of controls a browser feels incomplete without.
