Fixed. The address bar was losing typed text because of a wrong focus check in the chrome synchronizer.

**Root cause.** [browser.c](src/browser.c#L49-L57) skipped updating the address bar while it had focus, using `gtk_widget_has_focus(entry)`. Under GTK 4, a `GtkEntry` hands focus to an internal `GtkText`, so the entry itself reports `has_focus = 0` while the user is typing. The guard never fired, and every URI/load event (`notify::uri`, `load-changed`) replaced the entry text with the page URL. On a fresh launch — or any navigation while typing — the text vanished, which is exactly what "typing isn't working" looks like.

I proved it both ways with the real window: with the old check, typing `gtk interface` and then letting a page load turned the entry into `https://example.org/`; with the fix it stays `gtk interface`, and once focus leaves the bar the URL follows the page again (`https://example.net/`).

**Related hardening in [omnibox.c](src/omnibox.c):**
- The list's scrolled area is no longer focusable, so the suggestion list can't take keyboard focus from the entry.
- Focus is re-asserted on the entry after the list opens.
- Clicking the bar always refocuses the entry, even when the list is already open.

**Validation:** `meson compile -C build` is clean, `gcc -Wall -Wextra -Werror` over all sources is clean, and both probes (focus and text-overwrite, old vs. new guard) were run against the real window and then deleted.

**Limitation:** I could reproduce and fix the text-loss path, but I couldn't feed real keystrokes into a native window in this session. GTK's Broadway backend renders the app and accepts clicks, yet its daemon drops keyboard events before GTK sees them — a minimal one-entry control app showed the same, so that rig can't judge typing. The entry demonstrably owns focus (`GtkText`) while the list is open, and the only code path that could discard typed text is the one fixed above.

Please run `development/test.sh` again; if a character ever still fails to appear, note whether the list was open and I'll instrument the key path directly.
