The new tab page is now built from GTK widgets instead of an HTML document in the web view.

![Native new tab page](screenshots/newtab-native.png)

**Structure.** Each tab is now a `GtkStack` holding two pages: the native start page and the `WebKitWebView`. Both live in [tab.c](src/tab.c), which owns the stack and exposes `tab_show_web()` / `tab_showing_web()`; the browser chrome only ever asks the tab which page is on top. [pages.c](src/pages.c) no longer emits any HTML for the start page — it builds a `GtkScrolledWindow` containing the themed app icon, a `title-1` "Indigo" wordmark, an optional dim "Suggested" caption, and a `GtkFlowBox` of flat `GtkButton` tiles. Each tile is a 48px image plus an ellipsized label; the image is a `GdkTexture` rendered once per host with Pango/Cairo as a coloured rounded square and the host's initial (colour derived deterministically from the host name), so tiles stay distinct without favicons. Navigation, the address bar and Reload are all driven off the visible stack page: a start page has no URL, so the entry is empty and Reload is insensitive.

**Navigation.** Because the start page is no longer a web document, Back had stopped working after the first visit. The tab now records that its start page sits behind the current page, so Back from the first site returns to the native page (title reverts to "New Tab", URL clears) and Forward brings the site back. Chrome updates in [browser.c](src/browser.c) read the tab's stack rather than the notebook child, and the omnibox's tab list ([omnibox.c](src/omnibox.c)) resolves tabs from the notebook page properly instead of casting it to a web view.

**Verified by running it**, not just compiling: `meson compile` and a strict `-fsyntax-only -Wall -Wextra -Werror` pass are clean; I launched the binary on a headless Broadway display and drove the real UI — startup shows the native page with nothing focused over it, clicking the YouTube tile loads the site and updates title/URL/history, Back returns to the start page with Reload disabled and Forward enabled, Forward restores the page, `+` opens a second tab on the native page, closing that tab returns to the first intact, and Settings still loads its placeholder in the web view.

**Tradeoffs.** The grid is a snapshot taken when the tab is created, so an already-open start page won't pick up sites visited afterwards in another tab; the Settings placeholder is still HTML (out of scope for this change); and tile labels are single-line and ellipsized rather than clamped to two lines, since GTK's `gtk_label_set_lines()` would raise the minimum GTK version. Tile titles still come from the existing per-URL history, so one host can appear more than once if you visited different paths.

The test app, Broadway daemon and generated screenshot helper are all cleaned up; nothing was committed.
